 into lineages, and define cell-specific responses to smoking. Our analysis infers multi-state lineages that develop into surface mucus secretory and ciliated cells and then contrasts these to the unique specification of submucosal gland (SMG) cells. Accompanying knockout studies reveal that tuft-like cells are the likely progenitor of both pulmonary neuroendocrine cells and CFTR-rich ionocytes. Our smoking analysis finds that all cell types, including protected stem and SMG populations, are affected by smoking through both pan-epithelial smoking response networks and hundreds of cell-specific response genes, redefining the penetrance and cellular specificity of smoking effects on the human airway epithelium.", "affiliation": "Center for Genes, Environment, and Health, National Jewish Health, Denver, CO, 80206, USA.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "Cystic Fibrosis Foundation", "grantId": "BRATCH16I0", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "HL107202", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Jewish Health Regenerative Medicine and Genome Editing Program", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NHLBI NIH HHS", "acronym": "HL", "grantId": "P01 HL132821", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NHLBI NIH HHS", "acronym": "HL", "grantId": "R01 HL128439", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "HL132821", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "MD010443", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NHLBI NIH HHS", "acronym": "HL", "grantId": "P01 HL107202", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIMHD NIH HHS", "acronym": "MD", "grantId": "R01 MD010443", "orderIn": 0},